---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import ContentSection from '../../components/ContentSection.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import RelatedTools from '../../components/RelatedTools.astro';

const title = "Types of Angles - Geometry Guide";
const description = "Understand the different types of angles in geometry: Acute, Right, Obtuse, Straight, and Reflex angles with clear definitions and examples.";

const articleSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Article',
  headline: title,
  description: description,
  author: {
    '@type': 'Organization',
    name: 'Online Geometry Tools'
  }
};
---
<Layout title={title} description={description} schemas={[articleSchema]}>
  <ContentSection>
    <div class="w-full flex justify-center">
      <Breadcrumbs items={[
        { name: 'Guides', url: '/guides' },
        { name: 'Types of Angles', url: '/guides/types-of-angles' }
      ]} />
    </div>
    <div class="max-w-[768px] w-full mx-auto" style="margin-bottom: var(--spacing-xl);">
      <h1 class="display-lg text-[var(--color-ink)] text-center mb-12">The 5 Types of Angles</h1>
      
      <div class="prose prose-lg mx-auto text-[var(--color-body)]">
        <p>In geometry, angles are classified based on their measurement in degrees. Understanding these classifications is the foundation for almost all geometric principles.</p>
        
        <h2>1. Acute Angle (0° to 89°)</h2>
        <p>An acute angle is any angle that measures greater than 0° and less than 90°. These angles appear "sharp" or "narrow". If you look at an equilateral triangle, all three of its angles are acute (60°).</p>
        
        <h2>2. Right Angle (Exactly 90°)</h2>
        <p>A right angle measures exactly 90 degrees. It forms a perfect "L" shape. In geometric diagrams, a right angle is often indicated by a small square drawn at the vertex rather than a curved arc. Squares and rectangles are made entirely of right angles.</p>

        <h2>3. Obtuse Angle (91° to 179°)</h2>
        <p>An obtuse angle is wider than a right angle, measuring greater than 90° but less than 180°. These angles appear "blunt" or "wide".</p>

        <h2>4. Straight Angle (Exactly 180°)</h2>
        <p>A straight angle measures exactly 180 degrees. It looks like a perfectly straight line. It is effectively two right angles placed side-by-side (90° + 90° = 180°).</p>

        <h2>5. Reflex Angle (181° to 359°)</h2>
        <p>A reflex angle is an angle that is greater than 180° but less than 360°. When you draw an acute or obtuse angle, the "outside" or remaining portion of the circle makes up the reflex angle.</p>

        <div class="mt-8 p-6 bg-[var(--color-canvas-soft)] rounded-xl border border-[var(--color-hairline)] text-center">
          <h3 class="display-sm mb-4">Calculate Unknown Angles</h3>
          <p class="mb-6">Need to find complementary or supplementary angles? Use our free calculator.</p>
          <a href="/angle-calculator" class="btn-primary inline-block no-underline">Open Angle Calculator</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </ContentSection>
  <RelatedTools currentPath="/guides/types-of-angles" />
</Layout>
